<h2>Why minutes matter</h2>
<p>Someone who fills in a form is thinking about their problem right now. An hour later they’re in a meeting, or talking to whoever answered first. Leads reached within five minutes are far more likely to turn into a conversation than those reached the next day.</p>
<p>You don’t need a perfect reply. You need a <strong>prompt, human</strong> one.</p>

<h2>What to do when a lead comes in</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Read what they sent.</strong> It’s on the lead’s page under “What they sent”. Find the one thing they want.</li>
  <li><strong>Call if you have a number.</strong> A call within minutes feels like great service, not pushiness. Use the <em>First call</em> template.</li>
  <li><strong>No answer? Text, then email.</strong> Use <em>Missed your call</em> and <em>Couldn’t reach you by phone</em>. Both are under Replies on the lead’s page, filled in.</li>
  <li><strong>Set the next step</strong> for tomorrow as you mark the call done, so it doesn’t depend on remembering.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Making it fast without making it sloppy</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Start from a template, then change one line so it’s clearly about <em>them</em>: mention what they asked about.</li>
  <li>Ask one easy question. “Is now a good time for a quick call?” beats a list of five.</li>
  <li>Outside business hours, an email now and a call first thing is better than a text at 10pm.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Waiting until you have a full answer or a price. Reply first; answer properly once you understand the need.</li>
  <li>Sending a brochure instead of starting a conversation.</li>
  <li>One attempt and done. Most people need several touches before they reply (see <em>Following up without being pushy</em>).</li>
</ul>
